            <ArticleTitle>The importance of cognitive map placement in bile duct injuries.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>424-425</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Bile duct injuries often occur because of surgeon spatial disorientation. The psychological concept of cognitive map misplacement is a useful explanation of how this disorientation and injury occurs. Surgeons may find that using a &quot;bile duct time out&quot; is a helpful way to orient. Based on the mnemonic B-SAFE, they can use 5 subhepatic landmarks (B, bile duct; S, sulcus of Rouviere; A, hepatic artery; F, umbilical fissure; E, enteric/duodenum) to correctly place their cognitive map.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
